WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.780
Pixar is moving Onward, and critics are pretty
much on board.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.440
Reviews are coming in for Pixar's latest offering,
and the general consensus is that while it

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:12.919
may not be quite on par with some of the studio's
classics, it still packs plenty of laughs,

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.780
charm, and eye-popping animation.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:19.300
Onward tells the story of two elven brothers
who inhabit a once-magical world that seems

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.600
to have lost some of its luster.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.980
Together, they embark upon an epic quest to
discover if there is any magic left in the

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.779
world, magic which might help them resurrect
their departed dad long enough for them to

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.160
give him a proper goodbye.

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:34.460
The flick is directed by Dan Scanlon from
a screenplay he co-wrote with Jason Headley

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:35.400
and Keith Bunin.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:39.739
Marvel Cinematic Universe alumni Tom Holland
stars as Ian Lightfoot, while Chris Pratt

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.500
lends his voice to Barley Lightfoot.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:47.740
Among the supporting cast are Julia Louis-Dreyfus,
Octavia Spencer, Mel Rodriguez, Ali Wong,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:52.620
and Tracey Ullman, whose Fox variety show
introduced the world to The Simpsons in 1987.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.680
Even the flicks that don't live up to the
greatest entries in Pixar's catalog are still

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.031
going to be a cut above your average animated
fare, and the majority of critics felt that

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.180
Onward was a perfectly fine animated comedy
with the requisite amount of heartstring-tugging.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.340
Also, obviously, it looks spectacular; nobody
ever accused Pixar of cutting corners when

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.380
it comes to animation.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.200
Kristy Puchko of IGN wrote:

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.280
"...Scanlon has crafted an adventure that
gleefully flies high with its fantasy elements,

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.180
yet never loses touch with its grounded family
tale...

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.560
Plus, it has a sensational pace that keeps
the adventure rollicking and keeps the audience

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.299
on the edge of their seats... a terrifically
entertaining tale filled with humor, heart,

00:01:28.299 --> 00:01:30.840
and allusions that are sure to delight fantasy
fans."

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.270
Many reviewers were a bit more reserved in
their praise, alluding to Onward's failure

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:38.229
to hit the storied heights of Pixar's greatest
while still acknowledging its strengths.

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:39.780
CBR's Cynthia Vinney said:

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:44.740
"Onward simply doesn't have the same emotional,
dramatic or comedic power as the best of Pixar...

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:49.020
[It's] a minor entry in the Pixar canon...
a perfectly pleasant way to pass the time."

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.540
Overall, though, Onward received kudos for
the performances of Holland, Pratt, and Spencer

00:01:53.549 --> 00:01:57.759
in particular, and for hitting those familiar,
emotional notes we crave from Pixar.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.470
Den of Geek's David Crow remarked:

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.979
"Whatever its shortcomings, Onward is still
an amusingly clever piece of family entertainment

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.700
with a tangible heart at the center of its
book of spells…

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.600
This might be Pixar on an off-day, but even
then it feels like spending quality time with

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:11.340
a dear loved one."

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.640
A fairly strong contingent of reviewers were
quite a bit less forgiving.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.760
"I see you brought sustenance for our adventure!”

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.140
“No, It's garbage for the trash can."

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.800
They tended to peg Onward as half-baked, too
long, and... well, extremely weird in an off-putting

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:25.300
way.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.620
Mick LaSalle of the San Francisco Chronicle
wrote:

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:32.390
"Onward looks great, and it has a couple of
strong scenes that are gripping and even ingenious.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:36.730
But as a whole, the movie is misbegotten,
overlong and altogether unconvincing as a

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:37.840
story of familial love...

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.400
Long before its 114-minute running time has
elapsed, it has overstayed its welcome."

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.780
Mike Ryan of Uproxx was even less kind:

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:48.960
"This is a movie about two kids resurrecting
their dead dad's crotch and it's just the

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:49.600
WEIRDEST thing...

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.760
At one point in this movie I literally tried
to get emotional.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.000
If I'm watching nothing I can make myself
get there.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:56.740
Watching Onward, I couldn't do it.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.660
Every time I felt even a hint of emotion,
here comes the walking crotch."

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:05.800
Other reviewers slapped Onward with labels
such as "undercooked," "flattened and diluted,"

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.210
and "disappointingly pedestrian."

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:12.230
The consensus of Onward's negative notices
was summed up nicely by Josh Spiegel of Slashfilm:

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:13.160
"Onward is...

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:13.660
OK.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:17.080
The problem with Onward is that Pixar's original
films are incredible.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.580
OK is, simply, just not good enough."

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:23.890
The majority of critics, though, found Onward
to be a satisfactory-if-unspectacular addition

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:24.880
to Pixar's catalog.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.720
You can see for yourself whether the flick
hits your own personal mark when it drives

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.160
into theaters on March 6.

